WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.425
WHERE and HAVING both filter data but they work at
different stages

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:08.137
of an aggregate query WHERE filters individual
source rows before GROUP BY

00:00:08.162 --> 00:00:12.738
happens For example WHERE price is greater than or
equal to twenty-five

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:17.625
means that lower-priced sale rows never enter the
groups HAVING filters completed

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:22.838
groups after aggregation For example HAVING SUM
quantity greater than ten means

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:27.650
PostgreSQL first creates the category groups
calculates total quantity for each group

00:00:27.888 --> 00:00:31.675
and then removes groups that do not meet the
threshold A simple

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:35.262
way to remember the difference is WHERE asks which
rows should participate

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.738
HAVING asks which summary groups should remain At
the same query level

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.438
WHERE normally cannot test SUM COUNT or AVG because
those aggregate values

00:00:44.462 --> 00:00:48.725
do not exist yet HAVING can test them because
grouping has already

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:53.400
occurred Sometimes a condition could technically be
written in HAVING even though

00:00:53.412 --> 00:00:57.162
it belongs in WHERE That is usually less clear and
can do

00:00:57.188 --> 00:01:03.138
unnecessary work Row-level conditions belong in
WHERE whenever possible When

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.125
you see a filtering requirement ask whether it
describes an individual source

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:11.625
row or a calculated group That question usually
tells you whether to

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:12.762
use WHERE or HAVING
